  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\n- Isolated showers ending, then dry into next week\n\nRadar continues to show a dominant lake band moving south over\nLake MI, but appears to be just west of the Points. A weaker band\nof light rain showers from Lake Huron was moving SSW over \nPortland. All of the precipitation will continue to weaken tonight\nand Saturday morning. \n\nThe lake-induced precipitation has been a ramification of colder \nair brought south within the deep flow of an upper trough. \nBoundary layer flow is generally from the NNE and so that has \nlimited the areal extent of precipitation across the cwa. The \nmodels show a general rex block across the northern US, but also \nshow it breaking down this weekend as a strong short wave is \nejected eastward from the deep eastern Pacific trough. The result \nwill be ridging that will build over the Great Lakes and end the \nprecipitation. We'll see a gradual reduction of cloud cover as the\nweekend progresses and quite a bit of sunshine next week. \n\n- Much warmer next week\n\nH8 temperatures currently near -6c are progd to warm to around +8c\nby Thursday as heights rise under the upper ridge. That will\nresult in surface highs climbing from the upper 40s this weekend\nto lower to mid 50s early next week to around 60 toward the end of\nthe week Highs by next Thursday will be 10-15 degrees above\nnormal.",
